1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
This is TransitLab: a browser-native 3D
traffic world with an inspectable action boundary.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
This demo uses deterministic commands.
The real LLM remains shadow-only.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:25,000
I ask to add a bus.
The adapter proposes a versioned action.

4
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:35,000
Schema checks structure. Semantics check the scene.
Only then may execution update state.

5
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:50,000
Now I turn the signal red.
The 3D signal and state inspector show the change.

6
00:00:50,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Stopping the bus is an explicit command,
not an automatic traffic-simulation rule.

7
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Another validated command resumes movement.
The renderer displays approved state.

8
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:20,000
What if I ask for a snowstorm?
Weather is outside the current action protocol.

9
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:30,000
The request is rejected before execution.
The scene state remains unchanged.

10
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:40,000
The Event Log shows the command,
candidate, validation stage and rejection reason.

11
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:50,000
Compare the before and after snapshots.
A rejection is a visible, inspectable outcome.

12
00:01:50,000 --> 00:02:05,000
Runtime identity links the demo to its build.
The fingerprint also appears in each Event Log.

13
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:15,000
Today: five action types and a deterministic demo.
Not arbitrary language understanding.

14
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:25,000
Real-model candidates are evaluated without execution.
Language proposes. Validators decide.
